WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(gentle music)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:03.210
- [Narrator] One of the world's
biggest traffic jams isn't

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.370
on a road or highway.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.470
It's at the Panama Canal.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:08.610
By the end of August,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.220
a backlog of more than 200 ships waited

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.680
for weeks to pass through the waterway.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.470
Some were stalled for more than 20 days.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.310
- We handle about $600
billion worth of merchandise

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.230
through the Panama Canal.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:25.200
- [Narrator] And this vital
trade route is at risk.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.380
The cause a historic drought.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.230
The delays are rippling
through the global economy

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.760
as lower water levels cut

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.163
into shipping times and bottom lines.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:39.600
Here's how the Panama Canal
is adapting to a drier planet

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.240
and what those changes mean
for the global supply chains

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.413
and industries that depend on it.

00:00:46.064 --> 00:00:48.690
(bright music)
(explosion booming)

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.630
When the Panama Canal
first opened in 1914,

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:54.630
it was a game changer for
the shipping industry.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:57.660
- [Narrator] A little job
connecting the Atlantic

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:58.493
and Pacific.

00:00:59.689 --> 00:01:00.720
- [Narrator] For the first time,

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.600
cargo ships could save
about five days traveling

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.880
between the Atlantic and Pacific.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.890
Bypassing this treacherous journey

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.290
around the tip of South America.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:11.910
By connecting two oceans,

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.280
countries with vast distances between them

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.950
could now compete in Global Trade.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:18.780
But as the canal runs out of water,

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.300
canal operators like Ricaurte Vasquez

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.550
are scrambling to protect the root.

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.380
Vasquez is the head administrator

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.270
for the Panama Canal Authority,

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.760
which maintains and operates the waterway.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.950
- We handle about 55%

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:35.520
of all containers that go to
the United States from Asia.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.730
We do that in about
13,000 transits per year,

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.080
in regular times.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.030
- [Narrator] Ships like
this one are elevated

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.310
to about 85 feet above sea level

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:45.600
through a system of locks

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.793
and then lowered down on the other end.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:50.400
Every time a ship passes through

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.400
as much as 120 million gallons
of water are lost to the sea

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.180
that's enough to fill as many

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:01.410
as 244 Olympic sized swimming pools.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.360
All of that water comes from a system

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.680
of freshwater reservoirs and
rivers that depend on rainfall.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.240
- It operates on fresh water,

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:10.710
which is a major difference,

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:14.280
compared to any other major
waterway in the world.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.780
We cannot pump water from
the oceans into the lake.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:19.650
- [Narrator] Even though Panama

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.990
is the world's fifth rainiest country,

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:25.140
it no longer has a steady
supply of fresh water.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.030
- Over the last 20 years,

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:30.240
there is a downward trend
in water precipitation.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.090
We were 89 feet over sea level.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:36.420
Nowadays, we are 79 feet over sea level,

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.090
so that is a 10 feet reduction

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:42.690
on the lake level in a
relatively sizable lake.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:44.340
- [Narrator] Lower water
levels are a problem

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.230
for cargo ships that sometimes carry

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:49.353
as many as 16,000 containers at a time.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:51.390
If ships are too heavy,

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.850
they risk getting trapped in the canal.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:56.610
- We have to introduce draft restrictions,

00:02:56.610 --> 00:03:00.240
which imposes that every
vessel has to come lighter

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.820
or with less cargo in order to sail

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.290
through the Panama Canal.

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.570
- [Narrator] A ship's draft
limit is the minimum depth

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:09.300
of water a vessel can safely navigate.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.850
This decides how much
cargo a vessel can carry

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:13.650
without getting stuck.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.140
As drought conditions
worsened over the summer,

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.490
the waterway reduced its draft
limit from 50 feet to 44.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:22.950
- For a container vessel, on the average,

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:27.950
every feet of draft represents
about 300 to 350 containers.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:29.603
- [Narrator] Since may,

00:03:29.603 --> 00:03:32.160
large container vessels like this one

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:36.420
have had to reduce cargo
loads by as much as 25%.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:40.200
- That of course, means that
we can offer less capacity

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.540
to our customers as a result of this.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.040
- [Narrator] Lars Oestergaard Nielsen

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.430
is the Head of Customer Delivery
in the Americas at Maersk.

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.980
The world's second largest
cargo shipping carrier

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:51.780
in terms of capacity.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.900
- We actually sail extra
ships to fill in the blanks,

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.210
because we lose this six feet of water,

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:00.385
we lose an opportunity to load some cargo.

00:04:00.385 --> 00:04:01.770
(engine revving)

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:03.420
- [Narrator] Other ship
owners are adjusting

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:07.230
to the restrictions by offloading
their cargo onto trains.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:08.910
The boxes are unloaded from ships

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.240
on one side of the canal,
moved by rail and returned

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:15.000
to the vessels before they
continue their voyage.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.240
But the system doesn't
work for all cargo ships.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:22.410
- If you are a bulk carrier,
you taking coal, grain,

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:23.880
there is no way to unload.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:27.450
So you have to come light and
you come light from origin,

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:29.850
then you look for another port destination

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:31.140
to increase your cargo

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.300
and then take it to the final destination.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.520
- [Narrator] In an effort
to save even more water,

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.290
the canal has limited the amount

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:40.470
of vessels that are allowed
to pass through each day,

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:41.880
but fewer ships traveling

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.840
through the canal, means longer
wait times and higher tolls.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.970
Shipping executives say container vessels,

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:51.480
now pay around $400,000
to cross the waterway.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.340
That includes a fresh water surcharge

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:56.790
of more than 10%, meaning
the lower the water levels,

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:59.040
the more shipping companies have to pay.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.410
- So far, what we have tried is to reduce

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:05.340
from 36 transits per day
to 32 transits per day.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.730
So there are a lot less
spaces to have an option

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:10.380
for standby customers.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:14.100
Of the 30 slots or 32 slots that we offer

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:17.250
about 28 of them are already booked.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:18.900
- [Narrator] Container
ships that are able to book

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.180
in advance, typically aren't impacted

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:22.890
by these types of bottlenecks,

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:25.413
but vessels that aren't on fixed routes,

00:05:25.413 --> 00:05:27.330
like bulk and gas carriers

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:31.770
that move cargo on short notice
face the longest wait times.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:32.970
The delays for these types

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:37.350
of non-book vessels
increased 280% since June.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.720
That's according to project44.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.060
A supply chain visibility platform.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:43.440
On top of the delays,

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.130
bulk carriers are also
facing higher auction fees.

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:50.160
- There are now an opportunity
to go into an auction

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.190
where you can basically
bid for getting access

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:57.450
to these limited time slots
that were not booked early.

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:00.810
Now those auctions have
gone the highest I have seen

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:05.220
and the highest number we have
seen is a level of $900,000.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:07.020
- [Narrator] To make up
for the higher costs,

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:08.730
ship owners are charging customers

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:12.060
about $600 more per cargo container.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.100
- There's no doubt that
the canal is very important

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:15.270
for global trade.

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:16.560
If the canal was not there

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:20.010
then that would essentially add more cost.

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:21.330
It would add delays.

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:23.340
- [Narrator] Disruptions
in the canal's operations

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.770
could hurt southern hemisphere exporters

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:27.390
and northern importers.

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:31.260
Brazilian meat, Chilean wines
and bananas from Ecuador

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:33.660
are regularly shipped across the canal.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:35.400
Copper from Chile and natural gas

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:38.850
from the US Gulf Coast
transit the waterway too.

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:40.350
It's also one of the fastest

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:42.450
and least expensive ways to move grain

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:44.550
and other agricultural commodities,

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:47.700
which leave the port of
New Orleans for China.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:51.390
About 73% of all the traffic
passing through the canal

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:55.200
is either headed for or
leaving the United States.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.960
But shipping executives say
increasing transit times

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.510
and costs are leading some bulk carriers

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:02.460
to seek out alternate routes.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.040
Vessels carrying products
to customers in Asia,

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:06.930
may sail through the Suez Canal

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:08.640
or around the Cape of Good Hope,

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:10.890
adding one to two weeks to the journey.

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:13.290
- It's basically your
products that's in break bulk

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:14.430
or in tanker.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:15.540
So this could be oil,

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:19.950
it could be natural gas,
it could be grain products.

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:21.120
- [Narrator] For now, Maersk

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:24.180
and other large container
ship companies have no plans

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:27.000
to divert their core
shipments away from Panama.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:29.520
Still, executives say,
it's not off the table

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.053
if drought conditions worsen.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:34.860
- Yes, we have a problem.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:38.100
We acknowledge this will
impair your operation,

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:41.070
but in order to minimize
the adverse effect of it,

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:42.780
we are providing the information,

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:45.150
so you make the best decision based

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.610
on real data on real-time.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:49.440
- [Narrator] The canal's future now hinges

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:52.500
on $2 billion plan to divert as many

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:55.260
as four additional
rivers into the waterway.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:58.950
That's an addition to the three
rivers that already feed it.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:01.260
But adding new reservoirs is a costly

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:03.150
and lengthy undertaking.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:04.620
New water would have to come

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:07.110
from watersheds that are
farther from the canal,

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:10.830
requiring the construction
of tunnels as well as dams.

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:12.750
River diversions also put pressure

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:14.880
on the region's limited water sources,

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:16.920
which support over 2 million people

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.950
and the surrounding rainforest ecosystems.

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:21.750
Ultimately, canal operators

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:23.550
are working to maintain a balance

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:26.340
between the water needs of
the canal, the environment

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:29.130
and Panama's growing urban population.

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:32.850
- We have to make provisions
to supply fresh water

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:37.050
for water consumption well
in advance economic growth

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:38.430
and population growth.

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:40.200
There is a slight competition

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:45.200
between water for transit and
water for human consumption.

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.080
- [Narrator] The Panama Canal
Authority is also investing

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:51.990
in new infrastructure
outside of the waterway.

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:53.730
- We are thinking about roads,

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:56.610
we are thinking about storage facilities,

00:08:56.610 --> 00:09:00.150
we're thinking about complimentary
services to be provided

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:03.420
for cargoes that can wait in Panama

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:05.340
to find the final destination.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:07.350
- [Narrator] These changes
were meant to be carried out

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:09.990
over 25 years, but administrators say,

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:12.330
drought and changing
weather patterns has cut

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.970
that timeline down to 10.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:17.940
Canal operators see this
crisis as an opportunity

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:21.420
to fast track critical
infrastructure buildouts.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:23.700
- The opportunity is now

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:28.530
because there is a high level
of awareness of the situation.

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:30.360
People have a sense

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:34.321
of what the value of not
having fresh water means.

00:09:34.321 --> 00:09:36.904
(gentle music)

